        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "¿qué significa? — \"what does it mean?\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Significar is Latin significare, and it takes apart cleanly: signum, \"a sign,\" plus facere, \"to make.\" To mean something was to make a sign of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took the whole family: sign, signal, signify, significant, and design — de- plus signare, to mark something out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the twin of the question you cannot ask yet. One asks for a word you do not have; this one asks for the meaning of a word you have just heard and did not follow. Between them they cover both directions, and either one turns a stranger into a source."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Point at the written word, or say it back, and ask."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five repairs, and the last one gets you meanings rather than words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
